.TH CCCLOSE 1 "2026-10-16" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccclose \- check whether a session's close left anything owing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccclose
.RB [ \-\-session
.IR id | path ]
.RB [ \-\-root
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B ccclose
.BR \-h " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
A session that finishes a sequence of work does the tidy-up and then declares
that it is ready to close.
That declaration is a claim, and it needs evidence: worktrees and branches put
away, commits pushed, pull requests merged, the session record written, the
board rebuilt, no claim left live, the floor green on what was pushed, and
reviews queued where they are owed.
A session that answers those from its own memory is answering from the thing
that drifts most in a long session.
.PP
.B ccclose
answers them from a fresh read instead: of the repository, of the GitHub host
through
.BR gh (1),
and of the session's own transcript.
It prints the standard outputs (the session id, its transcript, its time
window, the repo and its head), eleven checks in a fixed order, the
principal's close-out asks found in the transcript, and a one-line verdict.
It changes nothing.
.SS The session and its window
The session is
.B $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
unless
.B \-\-session
names another.
Its transcript is found as
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ * / id .jsonl,
looking in every project folder, because a session that moved into a worktree
still logs under the folder it started in.
.PP
The session's
.B window
runs from the first to the last timestamp in the transcript.
When the session is the one running
.B ccclose
(its id is
.BR $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID ),
it is still live, so its window runs to now: a commit made just before the
check can postdate the log's last line.
The session's commits, branches, worktrees, claims and pull requests are taken
to be the ones dated inside that window on this repo.
.B This is a heuristic,
and every run prints it: two sessions that worked the same repo at the same
time can each be credited with the other's work.
One signal is stronger: a
.B [~]
claim line that names the first eight characters of the session id is this
session's whatever its date.
Times print in UTC, because the output is evidence that gets pasted into
records, and records keep UTC.
.SS The checks
Each check ends in one of four states:
.B \[u2705]
pass,
.B \[u274C]
fail,
.B \[u26A0]
partial or unknown, and
.B \[u2796]
not applicable, which means its input is absent and the line says why.
A check never passes on something it could not read.
.TP
.B Worktrees put away
A linked worktree whose admin directory was created inside the window and
still exists fails.
Creation time is the directory's birth time; where the filesystem records
none, the worktree's age is unknown and the check warns.
.TP
.B Branches put away
A local branch other than the default whose tip is inside the window fails,
whether or not it was merged: a landed branch is deleted, so a merged branch
that still exists is not put away.
Each is listed as
.I merged, not deleted
or
.IR "not merged" .
.TP
.B Commits pushed
A commit from the window that no remote-tracking ref contains fails.
.B ccclose
does not fetch, so this is as of the last fetch.
.TP
.B PRs merged
An open pull request created inside the window, or headed by one of the
session's branches, fails.
Needs a GitHub remote and a working
.BR gh .
.TP
.B Session record written
The session log is
.I docs/SESSIONS.md
or
.IR SESSIONS.md ,
with a
.I sessions/
detail store beside it when the repo keeps one.
No commit in the window touching the log fails; edits that are only
uncommitted fail as
.IR "written but not committed" .
An index line with no detail file warns.
.TP
.B Board index rebuilt
A generated board is a
.I ROADMAP.md
carrying board.py's generated banner beside a
.I roadmap/
item store.
.B ccclose
runs
.B board.py \-\-check
(the repo's own
.I tools/board.py
if it has one, else the copy in the atelier checkout
.B ccclose
lives in) and fails when the index is stale.
A hand-kept board has nothing to rebuild and is not applicable.
.TP
.B No live claim left
A
.B [~]
line in the board's item files fails when it names the session's id prefix or
carries a
.I claimed YYYY-MM-DD-HHMM
time inside the window.
Other live claims are counted, not failed.
.TP
.B Floor green at the pushed head
The commit checked is the newest one from the window that the default
branch's remote-tracking ref contains, not the ref's head: a later push by
another session would be evidence for a different tree.
It is passed to
.B gh run list \-\-commit
as a full sha, because a short one silently returns nothing.
Per workflow the latest run counts.
A failed run fails, and so does a
.B cancelled
one: a cancelled run is not a pass, so re-run it on that sha.
A run still in progress warns as pending.
No run at all warns.
.TP
.B Reviews queued where owed
When a doctrine or tool path changed in the window (whichever of
.IR docs/method/ ", " docs/build/ ", " docs/decisions/ ", " tools/ ,
.IR instruments/ ", " skills/ ", " commands/
the repo has), a line carrying the review-queued marker
.B \[u23F3]
must have been added to the board in the window.
A miss only warns, because whether work earns a review is a judgement:
self-verifying work over reviewed machinery does not.
The session says which.
.TP
.B Workaround-shaped commands
The transcript's shell commands are read for the shapes of a workaround:
hooks skipped, signing switched off, hooks redirected, a force push, a hard
reset, a bare
.BR "git stash" ,
staging everything, removing an
.IR index.lock ,
and
.BR "gh pr merge \-\-admin" .
Some are legitimate, so a hit only warns; each is listed for the session to
answer.
.TP
.B Working tree clean
Uncommitted changes warn.
They cannot be attributed: changes a session did not make are the sign that
another session is live.
.SS Asked of this close \(em answer each
After the checks,
.B ccclose
lists the principal's prompts that hold a close-out instruction, as short
quotes with their UTC time, their channel
.RI ( turn " or " mid-turn )
and the ref
.BR cctranscript (1)
gives the same exchange.
The principal's prompts are chosen by cctranscript's rules (not every
mid-turn record is the principal typing), less records marked as meta and
text that is only a harness wrapper.
.PP
The match is a
.BR "keyword heuristic" .
A sentence is listed when it has a strong close phrase (close out, ready to
close, before you finish, tidy up, wrap up, leave it clean), or both a cue
(make sure, remember to, check that, record, update) and an object (session
log, roadmap, board, changelog, worktree, branch, PR, push, learnings,
defects, evidence, assumptions, decisions, questions).
It lists; it does not judge whether an ask was met.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-session " id" | path
The session to check: a session id, looked up in every folder under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ ,
or the path to a transcript
.I .jsonl
file.
Default:
.BR $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID .
With neither, the session is unknown and every check that needs its window
says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-root " dir"
The repository to check.
Default: the current directory.
Any directory inside a repo will do; its top level is used.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output: the same content, with each check's status as
.BR pass ", " fail ", " warn " or " na ,
uncapped counts beside the capped lists, and
.B null
wherever a figure is unknown.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
A one-screen summary.
.SH OUTPUT
The field set is the same on every run: every check prints, zeros print, and a
figure the tool could not read prints
.BR unknown ,
never 0.
Each listed set is capped at 25 lines, followed by the number not shown.
The last line is the verdict, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
\[u274C] not ready to close \[u00B7] 2 fail \[u00B7] 1 unknown or partial \[u00B7] 6 pass \[u00B7] 2 not applicable \[u00B7] 3 ask(s) to answer
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ * / id .jsonl
Session transcripts, read only and a line at a time.
.TP
.I docs/SESSIONS.md \fRor\fP SESSIONS.md
The session log, with its
.I sessions/
detail store.
.TP
.I docs/ROADMAP.md \fRor\fP ROADMAP.md
The board index, with its
.I roadmap/
item store when the board is generated.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID
The default session, set by Claude Code in every session's shell.
.TP
.B CCCLOSE_GH
The
.B gh
executable to run.
Default:
.BR gh .
The tests point it at a stub.
.SH EXAMPLES
Check the current session's close, from the repo it worked in:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check an earlier session, against another checkout:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose \-\-session 1edeadf7\-7675\-488a\-a722\-a97ab5098531 \-\-root ~/src/demo
.fi
.RE
.PP
Feed the result to another tool:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ccclose \-\-json | jq '.checks[] | select(.status == "fail")'
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No check failed.
Some may warn or be unknown; the verdict line says so.
.TP
.B 1
At least one check failed.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown flag, a flag with no value, or a
.B \-\-root
that is not a directory.
.SH NOTES
.B ccclose
is read-only.
It never commits, pushes, fetches, removes a worktree or edits a file.
Every git call runs with
.BR \-\-no\-optional\-locks ,
because a plain
.B git status
refreshes the index under
.IR index.lock ,
and on a checkout another session shares, that write can collide with its
commit.
Its only network use is
.BR gh ,
and only when
.B gh
is installed and
.B gh auth status
succeeds; otherwise the pull request and floor checks are unknown.
.PP
It does not check whether the session's findings (defects, learnings,
evidence, assumptions) reached the record.
That is a reading of meaning, not a fact a fresh read can prove; the session
record check proves a record was written, and the asks list puts the rest to
the session.
.PP
The transcript format is internal to Claude Code and can shift between
releases; the classifiers are cctranscript's, so a fix there reaches both.
The design of record is
.IR instruments/ccclose.design.md .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cctranscript (1),
.BR ccrepo (1),
.BR ccarchive (1),
.BR gh (1),
.BR git-worktree (1)
